@model EmployeeCreateViewModel

<h1>@T["Add employee"]</h1>

<p class="text-muted">
    @T["Everybody is added as prospective, whatever their join date. Starting work is a dated decision somebody makes afterwards — which is what lets the record say 'hired, never turned up'."]
</p>

<form method="post" asp-action="Create" id="employee-create">
    <div asp-validation-summary="ModelOnly" class="text-danger"></div>

    <div class="mb-3">
        <label class="form-label" asp-for="Code">@T["Employee code"]</label>
        <input class="form-control" asp-for="Code" required />
        <span asp-validation-for="Code" class="text-danger"></span>
        <div class="form-text">
            @T["Unique in this tenant, including against employees who have left, and never changed afterwards: every recipe, export and integration names this person by it. Letters, digits, hyphens, underscores, dots or slashes — a plain payroll number is fine."]
        </div>
    </div>

    @* English required, Arabic optional unless the tenant says otherwise. The tag helper marks the
       English input required and never the Arabic one, because a tenant setting is not something a
       tag helper can see and the server is the authority either way. ADR-0003's addendum. *@
    <workmate-bilingual for-en="NameEn" for-ar="NameAr" label="@T["Name"]" />

    <div class="row">
        <div class="col-sm-6 mb-3">
            <label class="form-label" asp-for="JoinDate">@T["Join date"]</label>
            <input class="form-control" asp-for="JoinDate" type="date" required />
            <span asp-validation-for="JoinDate" class="text-danger"></span>
        </div>
        <div class="col-sm-6 mb-3">
            <label class="form-label" asp-for="DateOfBirth">@T["Date of birth"]</label>
            <input class="form-control" asp-for="DateOfBirth" type="date" />
            <span asp-validation-for="DateOfBirth" class="text-danger"></span>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="row">
        <div class="col-sm-6 mb-3">
            @* Bound to the managed nationality list. The core still stores a two-letter ISO code
               and is still beyond the form designer's reach — what the binding changes is which
               control edits it. A tenant that has not applied the standard lists sees the text box
               it has always seen, because the control falls back to free text when the list has
               nothing to offer. *@
            <workmate-lookup for="NationalityCode"
                             list-code="@StandardLookupLists.Nationality"
                             label="@T["Nationality"]" />
            <div class="form-text">@T["A two-letter ISO country code. Statutory treatment depends on it."]</div>
        </div>
        <div class="col-sm-6 mb-3">
            <label class="form-label" asp-for="Gender">@T["Gender"]</label>
            <select class="form-select" asp-for="Gender">
                @foreach (var gender in Enum.GetValues<Gender>())
                {
                    <option value="@gender"><partial name="_GenderName" model="gender" /></option>
                }
            </select>
            <div class="form-text">@T["Left unspecified rather than guessed when nobody has said."]</div>
        </div>
    </div>

    @* THE FREE LINE-MANAGER PICKER USED TO BE HERE. D31 moved it inside the block below, after
       Position, Grade and Reports to, because that is where the answer comes from: an A–Z list of
       every employee in the tenant, sitting above a block that had just derived who the post
       answers to, asked the same question twice and got two different answers.

       On a tenant with no positions it is still exactly this control, rendered at the foot of the
       form — see the else branch below. D30's scope: a tenant that has built no positions sees no
       change at all. *@

    @* ---- the fixed "Job and organisation" block (D30, design note §2.1) --------------------

        OFFERED INLINE AND OPTIONAL. A new employee with no position is the state this slice exists
        to stop being normal, so the question is asked here rather than on a screen somebody has to
        find afterwards — and leaving it blank still creates an unplaced employee exactly as before.
        Required would break every tenant that hires before HR has built its position list.

        THIS FORM MAY ASK WHEN THE EDITOR MAY NOT, and the reason is one field above: the join date.
        Every write in this slice is dated and nothing is defaulted (CLAUDE.md rule 3); the editor
        has no date to offer and so links to the dated forms instead, while this one already asked.

        A TENANT WITH NO POSITIONS SEES NONE OF IT. IsPositionBased is false, the block is absent,
        and the form is the one it has always been — which is the whole of D30's scope.
    *@
    @if (Model.IsPositionBased || Model.ShowsStructureChooser)
    {
        <h2 class="fs-5 mt-4" id="create-job-heading">@T["Job and organisation"]</h2>

        <input type="hidden" asp-for="IsRefresh" id="create-refresh" value="false" data-refresh-flag />

        @if (Model.ShowsStructureChooser)
        {
            <div class="mb-3">
                <label class="form-label" asp-for="StructureId">@T["Structure"]</label>
                @* A flagged refresh rather than a save, the same as the Placement screen's: without
                   it, choosing a structure ran the whole save path and asked for an employee code
                   nobody had been given the chance to type yet. *@
                <select class="form-select" asp-for="StructureId" id="create-structure"
                        onchange="document.getElementById('create-refresh').value='true'; this.form.submit();">
                    <option value="">@T["Choose a structure…"]</option>
                    @foreach (var structure in Model.Structures)
                    {
                        <option value="@structure.StructureId" selected="@(structure.StructureId == Model.StructureId)">
                            @structure.Name (@structure.Code)
                        </option>
                    }
                </select>
            </div>
        }
        else
        {
            <input type="hidden" asp-for="StructureId" />
        }

        @if (Model.IsPositionBased)
        {
            @* Active as at the join date, retired hidden, each option naming the post, the unit it
               sits in and filled/approved. The control already does all three — F5+F7 built it for
               the position's own "Reports to" — and re-asks when the date beside it changes.

               THE EMPTY OPTION IS RELABELLED, and it has to be. The control's default wording for
               it is "— reports to nobody —", which is the right sentence on the screen it was
               built for and nonsense on this one: the question here is which post somebody holds,
               not who they answer to. *@
            var noPosition = T["— no position yet —"];

            <workmate-position-picker for="PositionRecordId" label="@T["Position"]"
                                      structure-id="@Model.StructureId"
                                      as-at="@Model.JoinDate"
                                      date-field="JoinDate"
                                      refresh-on-change="true"
                                      allow-none="true"
                                      none-label="@noPosition" />

            <div class="form-text mb-3" id="create-position-hint">
                @T["Optional. Their department, division and company follow from where the position sits — nothing else to choose. Leave it blank and add one later from their record."]
            </div>

            @* D26 G, mode two: only this position's allowed grades, empty and disabled until a
               position is chosen, refreshed on every change of it. *@
            var gradeHint = T["Only the grades this position carries as its levels."].Value;

            <workmate-grade-picker for="GradeRecordId" label="@T["Grade"]"
                                   position-field="PositionRecordId"
                                   position-id="@Model.PositionRecordId"
                                   as-at="@Model.JoinDate"
                                   date-field="JoinDate"
                                   hint="@gradeHint" />

            @* Beside the control that caused it. The summary above is ModelOnly — a deliberate
               choice on this form, so that a message about the employee code appears under the
               employee code — and a field error with no span to render it is an error nobody ever
               sees. The pickers are tag helpers and bring no validation span of their own. *@
            <span asp-validation-for="PositionRecordId" class="text-danger d-block mb-2"></span>
            <span asp-validation-for="GradeRecordId" class="text-danger d-block mb-2"></span>

            @if (Model.LineManager.Mode == LineManagerMode.Override)
            {
                @* The exception — a dotted line, a matrix, a secondment. Rendered here rather than
                   in the partial because the picker binds to this form's own model; see the note
                   in _LineManagerChoice.cshtml. *@
                <workmate-employee-picker for="LineManagerEmployeeId" label="@T["Line manager"]" />
            }

            @* The chain, and ONE "Reports to" row carrying the post above, its state and whoever
               answers for it. The same partial the Placement screen and the employee page render,
               so four screens cannot come to disagree about who somebody answers to. *@
            <partial name="_JobAndOrganisation"
                     model="@(new JobAndOrganisationViewModel(Model.Organisation, Model.LineManager, Editable: true))" />

            @if (!string.IsNullOrEmpty(Model.PositionRecordId))
            {
                @* D26 I. Said before the save as well as after it: somebody filling this form is
                   the person who would act on it. *@
                <div class="alert alert-info py-2" id="create-access-roles">
                    @T["A position carries no permissions by itself. Review this person's access roles after adding them."]
                </div>
            }
        }
    }
    else
    {
        @* A TENANT WITH NO POSITIONS KEEPS TODAY'S FIELD, exactly as it was — D30's scope and
           D31's. There is no reporting line to follow, so the free picker is not a second answer
           to a question already asked; it is the only answer there is. *@
        <workmate-employee-picker for="LineManagerEmployeeId" label="@T["Line manager"]" />
        <div class="form-text mb-3">
            @T["Who they report to, which is a different fact from which unit they sit in — customers routinely have the two differ. Placement is set separately."]
        </div>
    }

    <button type="submit" class="btn btn-primary">@T["Add employee"]</button>
    <a class="btn btn-link" asp-action="Index">@T["Cancel"]</a>
</form>

@* The picker's search. Without it the control is still a working <select> of the first
   page; with it, everybody in the tenant is reachable by typing. *@
<script asp-name="workmate-employee-picker" at="Foot"></script>
@* And the two this slice added. The grade picker is the one that needs its script: it is empty
   and disabled until a position is chosen, and it is the script that re-asks when one is. *@
<script asp-name="workmate-position-picker" at="Foot"></script>
<script asp-name="workmate-grade-picker" at="Foot"></script>
